  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe use a hybrid optimization technique combining evolutionary search with local refinement to minimize the minimum overlap (Erds constant) for a step function on the interval [0,2]. The key idea is to represent the function with n_point pieces and evolve populations of candidate solutions, leveraging gradient information only in later stages to improve convergence. This approach balances exploration (finding potentially better solutions in the search space) and exploitation (refining around promising areas to escape local optima).\n\nThe central problem is non-convex and difficult due to:\n- The max operation in the objective function creating non-smooth regions\n- The linear equality constraint that complicates optimization\n- High dimensionality increasing the risk of getting stuck in local optima\n- The need for feasible solutions (integral=1, 0<=x<=1)\n\nOur approach addresses these by:\n- Using an evolutionary framework to explore the space efficiently\n- Incorporating repair mechanisms to maintain feasibility\n- Adapting the search strategy based on progress using time budgets\n- Starting with coarse piece counts to handle the high-dimensional space gracefully\n\nKey improvements over previous strategies include:\n- Dynamic repair handling directly within evaluations\n- Early termination support for time-constrained runs\n- Coarse-to-fine resolution transfer capability\n- Better separation of evaluation, repair, and search logic\n- Handling different piece counts through parametrization\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe optimize a vector h of length n_point_pieces representing function values at [0,2] divided into n_pieces each of width 2/n_piece. Constraints:\n- 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n- sum(h) = n_piece/2 (ensuring integral is 1)\n\nObjective: Minimize C5 = dx * max_k np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")[k] \nwhere dx = 2.0/n_pieces\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nThe algorithm runs these components:\n\nA. Initialization\n- Set budget to 100% of max time but allow early termination\n- Choose n_pieces dynamically from [10,100,270] or fixed if none supplied.\n- If parent supplied, use warm start via `initial_h_values`\n- Generate random starts with sum(h)\u2248n_piece/2 and clip to [0,1]\n\nB. Search subcomponents:\n1. Evaluator\n- Returns objective and feasibility error\n- Clips inputs to [0,1] and scales sum to n_piece/2 via repair\n\n2. What's best\n- Track best value, pieces, and time consumed\n\n3. Iterate\n- Split time between exploration and refinement\n- Prefer population-based evolutionary search with dynamic repair\n\n4. Repair\n- Uses systematic sum adjustment and bounds enforcement\n- Priority adjustment based on available adjustment space\n\n5. Search schedule\n- Use DE + Repair if inside time budget\n- Reserve 10% for fallback simplex method\n- Track best solution continuously\n\n6. Time management\n- Allocate ~80% time to exploration\n- Switch to targeted local search if plateauing detected\n\n7. Population management\n- Maintain diversity using adaptive mutation\nDefine and return results.\n\nD. Local refinement (fallback)\n- If under budget, use Nelder-Mead from best point\n- Repair inputs first and enforce hard constraints\n- Defined as hybrid function for direct application\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Primary: DE with adaptive repair\n- Secondary: Nelder-Mead with random restarts\n- Termination triggers:\n  - Time limits progress\n  - Small improvement windows\n  - Fallback: Repair any valid solution found during coarser piece count\n\n5. Budget estimation\n- Evaluation time ~O(n_pieces*log(n_pieces))\n- Initial variation ~1000-10000 value calc/s for n=70\n- Store dependencies externally to limit code size\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Repair input before evaluation\n- Monitor n_piece across levels (20,70,270)\n- Continue search even post-initialization\n- Return any feasible solution found under budget",
